From reading the code: Only one golem may be created per square.
Sorting polyfodder does not influence the likelyhood, but you can
influence from which material. If you want to protect your non-gem
fodder from golem creation, first drop a bazillion of individually
named rocks, then dump it all on the square. You will get a clay
golem, which uses up only gem material.

If more than one object wants to create a golem, the new golem type
simply replaces the old one because poly_zapped is a global variable
from the point of view of the subroutine *fhito =3D=3D bhito called in
zap.c, line 1736. Large stacks, i.e. multiple objects with the same
inventory letter, do increase the chance of shuddering and creating a
golem (zap.c, line 1016), but this does not apply to many individual
objects on the same square. Only after deciding on the golem, the game
sets out to use up that golem's input material (zap.c, line 1739). So
the chance of getting a golem is not affected by the ordering of your
polyfodder. However, the last object shuddered does determine which
material used up to make the golem. I believe items dropped onto a
pile are added to the beginning of the internal list, so you want to
drop the prospective victim material first.


@Neanderthal: Beams of polymorph do not bounce. Only rays do, i.e.
those with an animation showing where they travel.

@Doug Freyburger: Monsters will happily pick up cross-aligned
artifacts, even quest artifacts, but they will not wield or wear them.
(Silver haters won't pick up silver artifacts, though.)

